# Copy to `.dev.vars` (git-ignored). `wrangler dev` reads it as Worker secrets/vars; the
# db:*/seed scripts load it with dotenv-cli. Never put alternate credentials in comments.

# --- Required ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local overrides of wrangler.toml [vars] (which are the PRODUCTION values). Without these
# wrangler dev would run as APP_ENV=production with the prod APP_URL, and cookie-authed POSTs
# from the Vite origin (:3000) would fail CSRF.
APP_ENV=development
# APP_URL follows the UI port: change it together with DEV_UI_PORT below.
APP_URL=http://localhost:3000
# Launch is single-tenant (the tomls say so too). wrangler dev reads the toml; the seed reads THIS,
# so it creates the one tenant (named after APP_NAME) rather than the multi-tenant demo set.
TENANCY_MODE=single
# Dev ports (scripts/lib/dev-ports.mjs), for a machine where :3000/:3001 are taken (a Cloudflare
# Sandbox holds :3000). The shell wins over this file; unset means 3000 (Vite) / 3001 (wrangler).
# `pnpm dev`, Vite, the bootstrap and the seed read them; `pnpm dev:api` alone reads only the shell.
# DEV_UI_PORT=3000
# DEV_API_PORT=3001
# Extra hostnames Vite answers for, comma-separated (a sandbox preview URL's host).
# DEV_ALLOWED_HOSTS=
# Database driver (D35): overrides the toml's DATABASE_DRIVER (the DEPLOYED driver, "neon" in a
# fresh copy) for wrangler dev, the db:*/seed scripts and drizzle-kit. `postgres` = postgres.js over
# TCP to the compose Postgres, no proxy. `pnpm dev:db:up --neon` switches to `neon` through the local
# proxy (and writes NEON_LOCAL_PROXY); `--postgres` switches back. The bootstrap writes this line.
DATABASE_DRIVER=postgres
# Owner connection string for local Postgres (docker-compose.dev.yml). Deployed, the Worker reads
# the DATABASE_URL secret (neon) or the HYPERDRIVE binding (postgres); this is the local one, and
# what scripts/migrate.ts, db-roles.ts, seed.ts and drizzle-kit use.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://launch:launch_pass@localhost:5432/launch_dev
# The local Neon proxy (only with DATABASE_DRIVER=neon; written by `pnpm dev:db:up --neon`).
NEON_LOCAL_PROXY=
# AES-GCM key for OAuth tokens stored at rest. Generate: openssl rand -hex 32
OAUTH_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
# Feature flags, layer 1 (D30): comma-separated keys this machine ships AT ALL. Only consulted for
# a flag whose registry entry sets `environmentGated`; an ordinary rollout flag ignores it.
# Listed here because `wrangler dev` reads [vars] from wrangler.toml, which holds the PRODUCTION
# value — so a key you ship dark in production is dark on every developer's laptop without this.
# `kit-ai` brings back the kit's Chat, Agents, Knowledge and Search (hidden in Launch for now).
FEATURES_ENABLED=
# Agent human-in-the-loop (#17): how long a run parked on a person waits before it expires. Passed
# verbatim to `step.waitForEvent`, so it must be a Workflows duration string ("168 hours", "7 days").
# Workflows keeps a waiting instance for 30 days on Workers Paid but only 3 days on Free — past
# retention the instance is gone and the resume event can never arrive, so keep this under 3 days
# if you are not on Paid.
AGENT_INTERRUPT_TIMEOUT=168 hours

# --- Optional (feature-gated when absent) -----------------------------------------------
# Email via Resend. Absent → magic-link URLs are logged to the wrangler dev console.
RESEND_API_KEY=
# Comma-separated emails promoted to global admin on their first *verified* login.
BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_EMAILS=
# OAuth providers: leave blank to hide the button. Redirect URI is derived from APP_URL:
#   {APP_URL}/auth/{provider}/callback
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
MICROSOFT_CLIENT_ID=
MICROSOFT_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Any OpenID Connect issuer (Keycloak, Okta, Entra single-tenant, Auth0…) — SETUP.md "Sign in with
# any OIDC issuer". Blank OIDC_ISSUER = no SSO button. Redirect URI: {APP_URL}/auth/oidc/callback.
# OIDC_ISSUER must equal the issuer's `issuer` value exactly (trailing slash included if it has one).
# Deployed, OIDC_ISSUER/CLIENT_ID/LABEL/SCOPES and AUTH_OIDC_ONLY are [vars] in BOTH tomls;
# OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET is a secret (omit it for a public client — PKCE only).
OIDC_ISSUER=
OIDC_CLIENT_ID=
OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=
OIDC_LABEL=
OIDC_SCOPES=
# true = the login page goes straight to the issuer and hides the other methods (needs the two above).
AUTH_OIDC_ONLY=
# true = trust the issuer's email when it omits `email_verified` (single-tenant Entra does). Default
# false: such a sign-in is refused. An explicit `email_verified: false` is refused either way.
OIDC_TRUST_EMAIL=
# AI layer (Phase 3): platform default key; tenants may override in Settings → AI. Optional — without
# it chat falls back to Workers AI through the [ai] binding (zero key, billed to the Cloudflare account).
# Launch P3: coding sessions spend this key too (through the model proxy — it never enters a
# sandbox) unless the Setup page holds an `anthropic_api_key` credential.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# §18.22: the OpenAI key Codex sessions on Launch's account spend (through the egress — never in a
# sandbox) unless the Setup page holds an `openai_api_key` credential. Unused until Codex is wired.
OPENAI_API_KEY=
EMBEDDINGS_API_KEY=
# Evals only (D33, docs/EVALS.md): `pnpm eval --provider fireworks|gemini` (and `--judge-provider`)
# read these; the Worker never does, which is why they are comments here and not bindings.
# FIREWORKS_API_KEY=
# GEMINI_API_KEY=
# AI tracing (D32). Spans ALWAYS land in the local `ai_spans` table (`pnpm cli traces list`);
# these only add an OTLP backend. Langfuse: both keys are enough (preset auto-selected, Langfuse
# Cloud unless OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT points elsewhere). Phoenix locally:
#   docker run -p 6006:6006 arizephoenix/phoenix  →  OBSERVABILITY_PRESET=phoenix,
#   OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:6006 (protobuf by default — Phoenix takes nothing else).
LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY=
LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY=
OBSERVABILITY_PRESET=
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=
# Extra request headers, k=v,k=v (URL-encode values) — e.g. authorization=Bearer%20<key>
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS=
# Link-out for `traces show`: {traceId} is substituted.
OBSERVABILITY_TRACE_URL=

# --- Coding sessions (Launch P3, docs/SESSIONS-LOCAL.md) --------------------------------
# Under wrangler dev a session's sandbox is always a local Docker container, and its database is
# always a real Neon branch of the app's project (Neon connected in Setup). `local` swaps only the
# repo: the local git server instead of GitHub. Only with APP_ENV=development: loadConfig refuses
# `local` anywhere else. The tomls say `cloud`.
SESSION_BACKEND=local
# Preview origins: {label} becomes <port>-<shortId>-<token>; *.localhost resolves to 127.0.0.1.
SESSION_PREVIEW_URL=http://{label}.localhost:3001
# The local git server sessions clone from and push to (`pnpm sessions:local-git serve`).
SESSION_LOCAL_GIT_URL=http://localhost:9420
# Workspace backups for a cold resume (docs/SESSIONS-LOCAL.md § Suspend and resume): off | binding
# | presigned. Unset = binding under APP_ENV=development (the archive goes through the Durable
# Object into the local BACKUP_BUCKET); `off` if a restore is slower than a clone on your machine.
# This governs THIS Worker's containers (local Docker). A session on the REMOTE sandbox host is
# backed up `presigned` (the container moves the archive over presigned R2 URLs) whenever the
# account id below is set and this is not `off` — the host needs its R2 secrets too
# (docs/SESSIONS-LOCAL.md § Real containers from a laptop). Not a secret: your account id.
SESSION_WORKSPACE_BACKUP=
# CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=
# BACKUP_BUCKET_ENDPOINT=https://<account>.eu.r2.cloudflarestorage.com   # instead, for a jurisdiction bucket
# Where a session's CONTAINER runs is a PLATFORM SETTING now, not a var: Settings → Platform →
# Coding agents → Session sandbox — this Worker's containers (wrangler dev's Docker) or the remote
# sandbox host (a real Cloudflare container in the deployed `launch-sandbox-dev` Worker). `pnpm dev`
# makes both available whenever it can (Docker running; `wrangler login` and the host deployed —
# docs/DEPLOY.md § The sandbox host) and says which it could not. DEV_REMOTE_SANDBOX=0 in your
# shell skips the remote check. A leftover SESSION_SANDBOX_HOST=remote here is read once as the
# setting's starting value; delete it (docs/SESSIONS-LOCAL.md § Real containers from a laptop).
# How a session's container reaches the internet: allowlist (internet off, the egress allow-list)
# | open (internet on; only the model and git hosts intercepted, for their credentials). Blank =
# allowlist, which works under wrangler dev. The deployed tomls say open until Cloudflare's egress
# interception ends a container's stream after a WebSocket closes
# (docs/plans/sandbox-websocket-close.md). A remote host reads its own toml, not this.
SESSION_EGRESS=
# The Sandbox SDK's own Durable Object logs (every exec, every destroy, at info). `warn` keeps only
# what went wrong; `pretty` prints it on one line instead of a multi-line object.
SANDBOX_LOG_LEVEL=warn
SANDBOX_LOG_FORMAT=pretty
# Which coding agents sessions run (Claude Code, Codex), their models and whether people may use
# their own Claude subscription or ChatGPT plan are NOT vars: Settings → Platform → Setup → Coding
# agents (docs/CONCEPTS.md §18.22). With nothing set there, Claude Code on Launch's key only.

# --- Shared config and grants (Launch P5, docs/plans/p5-grants.md) ----------------------
# `local` records the names a push would put on an app's Worker and calls no vendor. Only with
# APP_ENV=development: loadConfig refuses `local` anywhere else. The tomls say `cloudflare`.
GRANT_BACKEND=local

# --- RLS (see docs/RLS.md) — leave as-is unless running the enforce spike ---------------
# App-role credential for scripts/db-roles.ts; username must equal APP_ROLE in src/db/schema/rls.ts
APP_DATABASE_URL=
